# Pest Testing 4

## Basic Usage

### Creating Tests

All tests must be written using Pest. Add new files beside existing tests in the directories this project already uses — match sibling naming, bootstrap, and fixture patterns.

### Test Organization

- Unit/integration tests: follow the layout already present under `tests/`.
- Browser tests: use the browser test directory this project defines when browser testing is enabled.
- Do NOT remove tests without approval — these are core application code.

### Basic Test Structure

Pest supports both `test()` and `it()`. Before writing new tests, check existing files in the same directory and match the project's convention.

@ignissnippet("Basic Pest Test Example", "php")
it('is true', function () {
    expect(true)->toBeTrue();
});
@endignissnippet

### Running Tests

- Run minimal tests with filter before finalizing: `{{ assist.binCommand('pest --filter=testName') }}`.
- Run all tests: `{{ assist.binCommand('pest') }}` or `{{ assist.cakeCommand('test') }}` when the project uses Cake's runner.
- Run one file: `{{ assist.binCommand('pest tests/TestCase/ExampleTest.php') }}`.

## Assertions

Prefer specific response assertions over generic status checks when using integration-style tests.

## Mocking

Import the mock helper this project uses before calling `mock()`. Check sibling tests for the correct namespace.

## Datasets

Use datasets for repetitive tests (validation rules, etc.):

@ignissnippet("Pest Dataset Example", "php")
it('has emails', function (string $email) {
    expect($email)->not->toBeEmpty();
})->with([
    'james' => 'james@example.com',
    'taylor' => 'taylor@example.com',
]);
@endignissnippet

## Pest 4 Features

| Feature | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| Browser Testing | Full integration tests in real browsers |
| Smoke Testing | Validate multiple pages quickly |
| Visual Regression | Compare screenshots for visual changes |
| Test Sharding | Parallel CI runs |
| Architecture Testing | Enforce code conventions |

### Browser Test Example

Browser tests run in real browsers for full integration testing when the project enables them:

- Use fixtures and existing authentication helpers instead of ad hoc database setup.
- Interact with the page: click, type, scroll, select, submit, drag-and-drop, touch gestures.
- Take screenshots or pause tests for debugging when needed.

@ignissnippet("Pest Browser Test Example", "php")
it('may visit the home page', function () {
    $page = visit('/');

    $page->assertSee('Home')
        ->assertNoJavaScriptErrors();
});
@endignissnippet

### Smoke Testing

Quickly validate multiple pages have no JavaScript errors:

@ignissnippet("Pest Smoke Testing Example", "php")
$pages = visit(['/', '/about', '/contact']);

$pages->assertNoJavaScriptErrors()->assertNoConsoleLogs();
@endignissnippet

### Architecture Testing

@ignissnippet("Architecture Test Example", "php")
arch('controllers')
    ->expect('App\\Controller')
    ->toHaveSuffix('Controller');
@endignissnippet

## Common Pitfalls

- Using generic status assertions when a specific assertion exists
- Forgetting datasets for repetitive validation tests
- Deleting tests without approval
- Forgetting `assertNoJavaScriptErrors()` in browser tests when JS errors matter
- Ignoring existing fixture/setup patterns used by sibling CakePHP tests
